/*
==========================================================================================
🧠 1. INTUITION (CORE IDEA)
==========================================================================================

Think of a locker system 🔐

    key (email/userId) → hash function → bucket (locker)

✔ Direct jump → No searching
✔ O(1) average lookup

Example:
    h("abc@gmail.com") → bucket 5

==========================================================================================
⚙️ 2. HASH FUNCTION
==========================================================================================

Definition:
    A function that maps a key → index (bucket)

    h(key) = index

Properties:
    ✔ Deterministic (same input → same output)
    ✔ Fast computation
    ✔ Uniform distribution (avoid clustering)

Example:
    h("abc") = 5
    h("xyz") = 2

==========================================================================================
🏗️ 3. HOW HASH INDEX WORKS (STEP-BY-STEP)
==========================================================================================

Step 1:
    index = hash(key)

Step 2:
    Go to bucket[index]

Step 3:
    Access data (via pointer)

Internal Structure:

    Bucket 0 → [ (key, row_pointer) ]
    Bucket 1 → [ (key, row_pointer), (key, row_pointer) ]
    Bucket 2 → [ ... ]

⚠️ IMPORTANT:
    Index stores → pointer to actual row (NOT full data)

==========================================================================================
⚡ 4. TIME COMPLEXITY
==========================================================================================

    Search   → O(1) avg 🔥
    Insert   → O(1)
    Delete   → O(1)

Worst case (collisions):
    → O(N)

==========================================================================================
🚨 5. COLLISION (VERY IMPORTANT)
==========================================================================================

Problem:
    Two keys map to same bucket

    h("abc") = 5
    h("xyz") = 5   ❌ collision

-----------------------------------
🔧 Collision Handling Techniques:
-----------------------------------

1. Chaining (MOST COMMON)
    Bucket 5 → [abc → xyz → pqr]

2. Open Addressing
    - Linear probing
    - Quadratic probing

==========================================================================================
❌ 6. DISADVANTAGES OF HASH INDEX
==========================================================================================

1. ❌ No Range Queries
        WHERE age > 25  → NOT possible

2. ❌ No Sorting
        ORDER BY → NOT supported

3. ❌ Collisions degrade performance
        Worst case → O(N)

4. ❌ Not Disk-Friendly
        Random access → more I/O

5. ❌ Resizing expensive
        Rehashing required

==========================================================================================
🧠 7. WHEN TO USE HASH INDEX
==========================================================================================

✔ Perfect for:
        WHERE id = 101
        WHERE email = "abc@gmail.com"

❌ Avoid for:
        WHERE price > 100
        ORDER BY queries

==========================================================================================
🆚 8. HASH vs B-TREE (INTERVIEW GOLD)
==========================================================================================

    Feature         | Hash Index     | B-Tree Index
    ------------------------------------------------
    Search          | O(1) 🔥        | O(log N)
    Range Query     | ❌             | ✅
    Sorting         | ❌             | ✅
    Disk Efficiency | ❌             | ✅
    Use Case        | Exact match    | General purpose

👉 Conclusion:
    Hash → Exact lookup
    B-Tree → Most real-world queries

==========================================================================================
🧠 9. WHERE HASH INDEX IS STORED (IMPORTANT)
==========================================================================================

👉 Can be stored in:

-----------------------------------
🟢 In-Memory (RAM)
-----------------------------------
    Example: Redis

    ✔ Ultra fast
    ❌ Limited size
    ❌ Risk of data loss (without persistence)

-----------------------------------
🔵 Disk-Based (Most Databases)
-----------------------------------

    Example: PostgreSQL, MySQL

    ✔ Stored on disk
    ✔ Frequently used data cached in RAM (Buffer Pool)

Analogy:
    Disk = Warehouse 🏬
    RAM  = Counter 🧾

==========================================================================================
🔥 10. WRITE AHEAD LOG (WAL)
==========================================================================================

🧠 Intuition:
    "First log → then apply changes"

-----------------------------------
⚙️ Flow:
-----------------------------------

Insert operation:

    Step 1: Write log
        "Insert key=abc into bucket 5"

    Step 2: Flush log to disk

    Step 3: Update hash index

-----------------------------------
💥 Crash Recovery:
-----------------------------------

    If crash happens:
        → Read WAL
        → Replay operations

    ✔ Data safe

-----------------------------------
⚠️ Why needed in hashing?
-----------------------------------

    - Frequent updates in buckets
    - Collisions handling
    - Rehashing

    → Risk of corruption without WAL

==========================================================================================
⏱️ 11. WAL PERFORMANCE IMPACT
==========================================================================================

❌ Writes become slower:
        INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE

    Because:
        Log write + flush required

✔ Reads unaffected:
        WAL only impacts writes

-----------------------------------
🚀 Optimizations:
-----------------------------------

1. Batching
2. Async flush
3. Group commit

==========================================================================================
🏗️ 12. TYPES OF HASHING
==========================================================================================

1. Static Hashing
    - Fixed buckets
    - Overflow problem

2. Dynamic Hashing

    a) Extendible Hashing
        - Directory grows
        - Efficient splitting

    b) Linear Hashing
        - Gradual resizing
        - No full rehash

==========================================================================================
🧪 13. REAL-WORLD USAGE
==========================================================================================

Example Query:
    SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 10

✔ Hash index is best

Systems:
    - Redis (heavily uses hashing)
    - DBs for exact lookups

==========================================================================================
🚨 14. COMMON INTERVIEW MISTAKES
==========================================================================================

❌ Using hash for range queries
❌ Ignoring collisions
❌ Overusing hash index
❌ Not mentioning WAL
❌ Saying hash works well on disk (without nuance)

